WEBVTT

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:03.500
An original cocktail, "Dancing in the Spotlight".

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.000
Created with the dance of the land's blessings in mind.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:08.130
Created with the dance of
the land's blessings in mind.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:44.680
Here we have a man
who has spent his entire life resisting.

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:48.810
He continues to struggle
against societal trends,

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:53.020
and doggedly refuses to take orders
from the crowd.

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:57.320
And he has only one wish...

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:01.370
to continue to be original.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.200
For instance, a riddle can be heard
being uttered from his lips

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.370
as he tips his glass.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:12.710
That is, what would a patron
who has gone through four malts,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:18.800
starting with Ardberg, want to drink next?

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:21.760
There are but a handful of people

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:23.510
who could answer this.

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:28.140
The answer needs a creative mind.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.360
And a human story within the glass.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.070
This evening's GIass of the Gods is

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:41.200
a passionate story born from
the drink which answers this riddle.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:43.950
A STORY IN A GLASS

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.710
I knew it wouldn't go down well.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:52.920
Just what should we have served him?

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:58.710
If you don't know the answer,

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.930
you're not fit to be a bartender.

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:07.470
The man walking the streets of Ginza
asking these riddles,

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.850
searching for tonight's Glass of the Gods,

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.350
is the screenwriter Shizuo Kasahara.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.980
He always turns up when he's getting
no work and he's hit a slump.

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:20.030
You try writing when
you've got writer's block.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:21.950
Cut the crap!

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:24.410
You think that's an excuse?

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:26.660
We're about to start shooting

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.950
and there's still no final script?

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.420
Get out here, Kasahara!

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:34.420
Kasahara!

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:38.550
What the hell is all this?

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:39.840
There's more and more of it.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:42.840
I don't care how much of a fan he is
this is getting ridiculous!

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.890
This is the film we made, isn't it? And...

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:48.470
what's this?

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:50.810
A Story in a Glass

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:52.690
A Story in a Glass?

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:01.320
The other person who will meet
the Glass of the Gods is

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.200
film director Ryuji Minehara.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:09.620
These two have been working together in
film since their student days

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.040
and are a famous duo in the film industry.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:15.920
But recently they have not
produced any real hits.

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:20.300
Good choice.

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:22.880
I see you get it, kid.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:25.130
I'm happy you like it.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.850
I sure do.
I feel like I'm back to my old...

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:28.930
You bastard!

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:30.640
I found you at last!

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:34.060
And I was just starting to feel like
I could write again, too.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:38.440
If you've got the time to be drinking
in a bar, go finish the damn script!

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:40.020
You get it, right?

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:43.780
If we can't shoot according to schedule,
the schedule for the actors is out,

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:45.240
and we go over-budget.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.780
We'll make a huge loss!

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:51.120
Hey! I'm playing a Iittle game
with this bartender.

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:54.410
Why don't you cool your jets,
shut up and grab a drink.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.580
A game?

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:00.960
Okay, kid. How about you start us off?

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:01.840
Yes, sir.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:06.340
If I may,
with your permission, I'll start.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:10.720
Let's say, we have a customer come in.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.430
They take a seat and say the following,

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:18.480
"A single malt, Ardbeg, straight."

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:20.020
Very well, sir.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:29.660
Delicious.

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:35.450
After which a Laphroaig,
then a Miltonduff, Glenburgie,

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:36.620
and then...

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:40.250
Can I get one last drink?

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:42.500
What can I get you, sir?

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:43.840
I'll have a mizuwari.

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:50.470
Now then, which whisky would you choose?

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:51.850
That is the question.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:53.890
What...?

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:57.390
You twit... That's for you to guess.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:02.020
Well, that's a tricky one...

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:06.070
I give up.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:09.410
Can I get a clue?

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:12.280
How about
"the birthplace of the single malt".

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:12.990
Ah...

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:16.160
The year was 1707,

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.670
Scotland was annexed
as part of the British Empire.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.250
The malt producing region was
divided into six main areas,

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:26.670
Highland, Speyside, Islay,
Cambletown, Lowland

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:30.630
and the remaining islands.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:35.970
They may be the same "single malt" but
the taste is quite different by region.

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:37.390
Absolutely.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:41.770
Now then, how about the Ardbeg
which we began our riddle with?

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:48.400
This is an Islay spirit, characterised by
its unique iodine nose and strong flavour.

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:52.820
Hey! That's like the one in your room...

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:54.280
Shut it and listen, would you?

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:57.160
Now for the Laphroaig.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:59.540
This brand also reminds us of iodine

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:02.500
and is nicknamed the King of Islay.

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:10.260
Our third bottle is a Speyside
called Miltonduff.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:15.640
In the small Speyside area, which sits
as though independent among the Highlands,

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:18.520
they produce the famous Macallan,
Glenlivet

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:21.770
and the world leader in terms of sales
the Glenfiddich.

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:25.900
Among those is our fourth bottle,
the now very hard-to-find

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:27.480
Glenburgie.

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:28.780
How does it taste?

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:34.450
They all have an individual taste
each one different from the last.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:39.580
Now, what do you think is the next drink
that ties these four bottles together?

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:46.840
If we're talking about the mizuwari
there can be but one...

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:48.840
I know!

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:53.590
The Famous Grouse.

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:03.310
How is it, sir?

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:12.530
Do you know what the most important
ability a bartender can have is?

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:17.160
The power of imagination
geared towards people.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.660
The power to create
a human story in a glass.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.840
The life a customer sat
in front of you sees in their glass.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:32.840
To sense that and write in their glass

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:35.220
a story called "spirits".

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:38.470
It isn't just bartenders,

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:43.020
all people who create
are fighting tooth and nail.

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:48.230
What I want to drink is a story
that echoes deep in my soul.

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:57.160
A STORY IN A GLASS
A story in a glass. Right...

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:57.660
A story in a glass. Right...

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:02.450
It's just like you to make up
a snobby riddle Iike that.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:04.200
You think?

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:08.880
But, for me, as a screenwriter,
it's an important one to have answered.

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:13.960
Kid, let him have the right answer,
like this one.

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:14.880
Certainly.

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:18.680
Enjoy.

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:33.280
Ballantine, 12 years old.

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:45.370
Ballantine, 12 years old...

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:48.170
But why is that the answer?

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:50.920
It's a key malt.

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:52.920
What's that?

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:54.630
Do you know?

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:58.800
Whisky was originally a resistance spirit.

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:01.850
In the 13th century, to avoid
England's excessive malt taxes

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:07.730
Scotland's distillers hid their secretly
produced whisky in sherry casks.

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:11.730
Over time, the whisky that matured inside

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:15.650
retained the scent of the casks
and their amber hue.

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:19.240
So, until then whisky was a clear colour?

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:21.780
And this is the colour of resistance?

00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:24.490
But what does that
have to do with "key malt"?

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:27.620
Say, do you remember this picture?

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:34.840
This is from when you and I were
in the film club at university.

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:37.760
Those were good times.

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:42.800
We got all excited about going to South
America to shoot revolutionary films.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:47.560
We did okay considering the cramped,
sweaty, dingy room we were stuck in.

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:49.940
Watch your mouth.
That's my flat you're talking about.

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:52.600
We were totally engrossed in our work.

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:55.400
I'd roll the camera and
you'd write the script.

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:58.320
We didn't need anything else.
We had everything we needed.

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:01.320
That's right.
We weren't Iooking to follow trends.

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:03.910
We kept on resisting with passion
as our ultimate weapon.

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:04.990
You ain't wrong.

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:07.830
But that one shot really messed things up.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:12.080
When we reluctantly took best idol for

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:14.290
film director and screenplay of the year,

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:18.010
it meant that we had to make
two, then three sequels...

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:22.050
I didn't want people thinking of that
garbage as my masterpiece.

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:31.140
Now, no matter what I write, I can't seem
to bring back the enthusiasm or

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:34.190
the fire in my belly
I once had for writing.

00:11:34.650 --> 00:11:37.020
Absolutely nothing is fun anymore.

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:41.240
It's as if I'm one of those
blended whiskies with no depth.

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:42.320
Blended?

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:43.570
That's right.

00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:47.830
Firstly, in contrast to malt types made
using heavily taxed malt,

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:50.410
they produced cheaper grain type whiskies

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:53.210
using corn or other grains.

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:59.630
So some clever fellow thought he'd mix
cheap grain whisky and expensive malt.

00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:04.380
Specialists called blenders produced
a great many varieties of mixes

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:06.970
and the blended whisky was born.

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:10.430
"Key malt" refers to the single malt

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:12.600
used as the main constituent
in that process.

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:14.690
Much like in films.

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:16.520
If the director is the blender,

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:19.150
the screenwriter and the cast
are the single malt.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:21.740
And this guy here is us, back in the day.

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:25.990
What are you trying to say?

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:29.450
This 12-year-old Ballantine here...

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:33.000
is a whisky that was blended
from these key malts by a blender,

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:36.210
to be drunk as a mizuwari.

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:42.210
The long road to its completion is obvious
as soon as you take one sip.

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:47.510
The passion these blenders have, dreaming
of the matured flavours

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:51.720
12 years down the line as they mix
the different whiskies over and over.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:57.690
It overflows in each and every drop,
creating the story in the glass.

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:02.440
It reminds me of the sight of
the two of us back then,

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:04.490
furiously wanting to make films.

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:15.410
But then, because of all this blending,
whisky's lost its original uniqueness

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:19.790
and has become nothing more than
brands without depth, made for the masses.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:23.130
I see what you're trying to say.

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:26.260
Yep. Our work back then was blended
with originality in mind,

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:30.140
like this Ballantine here.

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:32.430
But, if you Iook at our recent stuff...

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:36.480
So that blend without depth
is my recent work

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:39.020
and the blame for your inability

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:42.190
to write lies with me, the blender?
Is that what you want to say?

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:44.070
Is that why you did it?

00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:51.530
The originality in our work did start
waning after that incident,

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:52.910
I'll give you that.

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:55.790
But come on... Grow up, Kasahara.

00:13:56.540 --> 00:14:00.790
You can't live off the ideals and
passion you had when you were a kid.

00:14:02.840 --> 00:14:06.800
So you're suggesting we sell out for
a quick buck, like these blends?

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:10.510
Becoming an adult means
throwing away passion

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:13.220
and compromising with reality, then?

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:20.850
These last 10 years, our films
have been watered down oversold rubbish

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:22.230
with no sign of originality.

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:25.320
The result always fizzles out to nothing.

00:14:26.650 --> 00:14:28.950
What do you know about
the pressure I'm under?

00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:31.620
It sounds nice to Iook for "originality",

00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:35.160
but it's me, the director who brings
together indulgent actors and scripts

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:36.830
to make one unified work of art!

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:38.910
It's that very attitude itself...

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:41.960
It shows how little
you respected my scripts.

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:43.170
What?

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:45.210
If you're that bothered about originality,

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:48.300
why don't you go write a novel
or something on your own?

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:53.600
It'd suit someone like you who likes
these disgusting undrinkable single malts!

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:54.470
Screw you!

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:57.680
Without an ultimate script
as the base whisky

00:14:57.930 --> 00:14:59.730
who do you think would watch your films?

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:01.730
But to you the script is no more than

00:15:02.060 --> 00:15:06.070
another malt that adds unnecessary
ingredients to the mix!

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:12.360
I'm afraid you are both wrong.

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:13.120
-What?
-What?

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:16.660
The single malt is not
the only spirit of resistance.

00:15:17.290 --> 00:15:21.290
Blended whisky was also born
out of the passion of resistance.

00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:28.630
Blends are spirits of resistance,
just like the single malt?

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:29.760
They are.

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:35.640
It started with the continued levies
on malt whisky by the English.

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:37.930
To counter high taxation,

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:44.810
the Scottish produced grain whisky from
the likes of corn, rather than malt.

00:15:44.980 --> 00:15:47.150
They opposed the high taxes.

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:50.190
In the end, blended whisky was created

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:52.900
as another means of resisting
against the tax levies,

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:55.450
through the mixing of malt and grain.

00:15:56.070 --> 00:16:00.250
It may very well have been in order to
avoid paying those heavy taxes,

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:01.200
but still...

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:04.460
Whisky had been no more
than a local spirit.

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:08.500
But through blending, the distinctive
single malt taste became easier to drink,

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:11.090
and came to be loved across the world.

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:16.220
Maybe it was the new meeting
between grain and malt

00:16:16.430 --> 00:16:18.600
that created a new flavour.

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:21.310
Like your first meeting.

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:23.600
Meetings, you say.

00:16:25.480 --> 00:16:26.730
It's been forty years,

00:16:27.110 --> 00:16:29.230
but what have we accomplished?

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:32.400
I sometimes can't help think this...

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:35.240
I don't know if it's age,
or just willpower...

00:16:35.740 --> 00:16:38.490
but I don't feel the same passion
that I did back then.

00:16:39.490 --> 00:16:40.910
I knew it already...

00:16:41.370 --> 00:16:45.460
that it was me and not you
that had lost originality.

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:47.630
So, to try and get that back

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:49.460
I came up with that riddle.

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:53.920
I can't see
the story in the glass anymore.

00:16:54.090 --> 00:16:55.630
The truth is...

00:16:56.090 --> 00:16:58.470
I was surprised by the Ballantine's, too.

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:03.020
Take a Iook. They're laughing at us.

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:07.560
It just shows that
we'll never be like Ballantine's.

00:17:08.690 --> 00:17:11.070
It's over, isn't it?

00:17:13.110 --> 00:17:17.360
It is unfortunate that when
they begin to give up on creating

00:17:17.950 --> 00:17:20.620
that is when their flames of
passion start to disappear.

00:17:21.160 --> 00:17:24.450
However, the cocktail they will next try

00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:28.120
will begin to inscribe a moving tale
upon their souls.

00:17:28.670 --> 00:17:31.170
These are the curious
beginnings of Eden hall.

00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:35.670
Kid...

00:17:36.260 --> 00:17:37.180
Surprise me, would you?

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:38.430
Very well, sir.

00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:43.430
How about I offer you a small
token of my appreciation?

00:17:43.890 --> 00:17:45.680
Appreciation? For what?

00:17:46.890 --> 00:17:51.190
This whisky is considered quite intense,
even among the most unique single malts.

00:17:51.310 --> 00:17:53.230
The Lagavulin, from Islay.

00:17:53.780 --> 00:17:55.690
45 ml goes into the glass.

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:57.570
With 15 ml of lemon juice.

00:17:58.070 --> 00:18:00.700
Then mix in a small amount of honey.

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:04.160
Then 20 ml of Drambuie
goes into the ladle.

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:16.840
This drink would be shared around bonfires
by men who hunted snow grouse

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:19.050
out in the wintry wilderness.

00:18:20.140 --> 00:18:23.260
This cocktail not only warms the body,

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:25.430
but also heals the soul.

00:18:25.970 --> 00:18:29.600
This is precisely why all fall silent
before the flame.

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:33.980
The name of the cocktail created by adding

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:37.740
the flame of passion to characteristic
single malts is called...

00:18:39.530 --> 00:18:41.320
The Aberdeen Angus.

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:45.450
It is neither a cold nor a hot cocktail.

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:50.250
It has the aroma of peat,

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:53.000
tamed through the addition of
single malt and liqueur.

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:57.300
A taste neither of them
had experienced before.

00:18:59.220 --> 00:19:02.340
A small bonfire inside
a metropolitan building, eh?

00:19:02.550 --> 00:19:03.510
Ten years ago...

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:07.890
a young boy who came to Tokyo,
attracted by its bright lights.

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:11.520
Amidst those bright lights,
he saw your films,

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:14.520
and his soul was so moved it trembled.

00:19:15.110 --> 00:19:16.980
He thought, "It can be in another form..."

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:21.030
"...but one day I, too, want to have
a job that moves people's souls."

00:19:21.530 --> 00:19:25.530
"I have to make sure not to
lose that passion," he decided.

00:19:27.370 --> 00:19:28.790
And eventually the young Iad

00:19:29.370 --> 00:19:31.160
became a bartender...

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:34.630
It isn't just me.

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:38.380
I believe everybody is attracted
to some kind of bright light,

00:19:39.010 --> 00:19:44.050
living each day with the passion found
in the light hidden close to their heart.

00:19:46.050 --> 00:19:49.430
The Glass of the Gods,
Ryu Sasakura says this,

00:19:51.100 --> 00:19:53.730
The flames of passion
can be found in each glass,

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:56.940
in a bar, irrespective of the drink.

00:19:58.070 --> 00:19:59.360
A passion for flavour.

00:20:00.400 --> 00:20:02.490
A passion for service.

00:20:02.990 --> 00:20:03.820
And...

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:07.580
the passion for calming the souls
of weary customers

00:20:07.780 --> 00:20:10.160
resides within each one of them.

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:14.170
It's the same.

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:19.000
We put all our passion into our films,
whether they flopped or not.

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:21.760
Now if we just don't put out that flame,

00:20:22.050 --> 00:20:24.590
we should be able to start up
something new from today.

00:20:26.090 --> 00:20:27.140
Did you know...?

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:30.060
When facing the camera for a picture,

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:32.480
in Japan and America, they say "cheese".

00:20:32.930 --> 00:20:34.730
In Korea, it's "kimchi".

00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:37.150
In Germany it's "beer".

00:20:37.650 --> 00:20:41.230
And in South America,
they say the following...

00:20:42.280 --> 00:20:44.490
"Whisky!"

00:20:54.500 --> 00:20:58.250
A STORY IN A GLASS

00:20:58.330 --> 00:21:02.550
DIRECTED BY RYUJI MINEYAMA
SCREENPLAY BY SHIZUO KASAHARA

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:06.430
THE END

